("p", "The strongest attacks on each new object, stated as an adversary would state them. Against Law 0: an equilibrium observer might detect its bottleneck through a channel the reversibility criterion does not cover — a driven-but-sufficient source with positive entropy production and zero obstruction would leave the law standing but gut its converse; the simulation search of Prediction 7 is where this is settled. Against Theorem I: the conservation form is assembled from proved transport propositions, but the functorial statement — the one that would make “conserved” precise in the graded category — is blocked on the enrichment that Volume II named and this volume does not build; until then, the first law is a family of instances with a common bookkeeping, not yet one theorem. Against Theorem II: the equality analysis is proved only in the isometry case, and the pairing of the 0.361 relaxation with the 3/2 fatigue is a reading, not a derivation — the two laws govern the same loop in different regimes, but no lemma here derives one from the other."),
("p", "Against Theorem III: parts (a)–(c) are standard mathematics applied to corpus objects, and their novelty is the assembly; part (d3) is a conjecture whose witnesses are correlational — the metabolic arrow, the fatigue exponent, the replica gap, the ECOMOD window, and the record rate function all point the same way, but none of them entails the implication, and a lumpable but driven counterexample family is a live possibility. Against Theorem IV: the kink prediction could be smoothed by finite-L effects rather than refuted by them, which is why the prediction includes the L-ladder as its own control; the metabolic half is proved, the quantum half is proved only at the level of eigenvalue-crossing perturbation in the fragment, and the cross-substrate identity of the two response laws is an extrapolation with two data points. Against Theorem V: the meter presumes the affine model of the instrument family, and a hardware deviation measures the tomography before it measures the theory. Against Theorem VI: the independent-block discharge is the easy half, partly definitional, and must not be read as the multiletter theorem; against the one-wall hypothesis: it has no proof face, and it is stated so that its refutation is a well-defined task. None of the six theorems claims its open core; the cores are in Table 2."),
("p", "What this volume does not claim, finally, because the programme’s credibility rests on these denials holding. It does not claim BT1a — the obstruction-to-Hankel recovery remains the deepest open link, untouched here. It does not claim the cost-enriched graded category exists — it remains the load-bearing gap, and two of the six theorems (I and VI) would upgrade from assemblies to single functorial statements the day it is built. It does not claim any cohomological, homotopical, or higher-arity completeness for the obstruction datum — the monograph’s own scope guard stands, and DeepSeek’s Bridge Theorem 1 remains corrected, not vindicated. It does not claim the multiletter AAK theorem, the arrow-of-time theorem, or the graded small-gain equality — it claims discharged fragments, named lemmas, and falsifiable conjectures in their direction. And it does not claim that any prediction has been tested: the nine entries of Table 3 are offers, not results. The volume creates at the frontier; the frontier moves; the ledger records where it stopped."),
