("p", "The priority queue is ordered by information per unit cost, and the first three orders are cheap because the apparatus already exists. Order one: run the record-phase scan. The monitored circuits are solved, the closure is exact, the boundaries are certified at 0.233810 and above, the production cells at p = 0.16 sit a step above the quenched critical point, and the v23 L = 8 rung is computing now — a p-scan across the annealed chain with the exact exponent ladder attached is the fastest falsification or confirmation of this volume’s headline physics. Order two: the E. coli cycle-size scan — a new statistic (approach-to-asymptote against 1 − 0.697<super>k</super>) on panels that already exist. Order three: state and attack optic-Nehari for correlated composites — a named lemma with a precise statement, the one proof that would upgrade a published conditional theorem (7.11) and split a published open problem (7.13) permanently; it is the highest-value proof target in the queue because Volume II’s Continuation A and the graded-trace small-gain law both converge on it."),
("p", "Orders four through seven are the build-and-prove tier. Four: write the obstruction-spectrometer protocol as a short instrument paper — every formula is proved in the instruments corpus, the calibration table is exact, and the qutrit prediction is a clean experimental contract. Five: the arrow-of-time simulation campaign of Prediction 6, which either kills part (d3) cheaply or arms the theorem that Continuation B was pointing at. Six: the enrichment — unchanged in priority from Volume II, still the load-bearing gap, and now carrying two upgrade promises (Theorems I and VI) instead of one. Seven: BT1a, the deep identification, last as ever. The staging respects DeepSeek’s path with the same correction Volume II made: the bridge paper first, fifteen to twenty pages, but its skeleton now has meat that the earlier volumes could not give it — a proved fragment of Continuation A, a realized finite-case of Continuation B, a conservation law with instances across eight domains, and a physics prediction that a running computation can check this month."),
